From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ps4-gladius <ps4-gladius@invalid>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 11:55:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: debug output from a thread that is not Wine's.

The server died serving GTA IV's get_handle_fd, and the faulting instruction
was never in the handler:

    rip = ntdll+0x53173 = __wine_dbg_header   (debug.c:364)
    fault address 0x7c, rbx 0x78

get_info reads get_thread_data()->debug_info. get_thread_data is
pthread_getspecific, and the in-process server runs on a thread the server
creates itself (start_inprocess_server), which has none, so it answers NULL.
debug_info is an inline char[0x800], so NULL->debug_info is not a fault but the
offset itself, 0x78, and the first field read through it lands on 0x7c.

Any debug output from the server thread therefore killed the process. That is
why no server-side ERR has appeared in a log, and it means the crash was the
second fault: handle_syscall_fault was reporting an earlier one when the
reporting killed it.

Two reads, not one. __wine_dbg_header prints GetCurrentThreadId() on the line
after get_info's, which is PsGetCurrentThreadId, which is
get_thread_data()->tid at offset 0x1c -- so fixing get_info alone moved the
crash from 0x7c to 0x1c and no further. Both are here. The debug path has no
third: get_info and that id are its only reaches into thread data.

A thread without Wine's data gets the buffer the process uses before threading
starts, and no thread id.

---
diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/debug.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/debug.c
index fe747fe055..dcc89c48b2 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/debug.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/debug.c
@@ -68,7 +68,26 @@ static const char * const debug_classes[] = { "fixme", "err", "warn", "trace" };
 static inline struct debug_info *get_info(void)
 {
     if (!init_done) return &initial_info;
+#ifdef __PROSPERO__
+    /* ⚠ Not every thread here is one of Wine's. The in-process server runs on
+     * a thread the server itself creates (start_inprocess_server), and
+     * get_thread_data is pthread_getspecific, which answers NULL on it.
+     * debug_info is an inline array, so NULL->debug_info is not a fault but the
+     * offset itself, 0x78, and the first read through it lands on 0x7c:
+     *
+     *     SIGSEGV, fault address 0x7c, rbx 0x78, in __wine_dbg_header
+     *
+     * So every ERR the server ever tried to print killed it -- which is why not
+     * one of them has appeared in a log. Give a thread without Wine's data the
+     * shared buffer the process uses before threading starts. */
+    {
+        struct thread_data *data = get_thread_data();
+        if (!data) return &initial_info;
+        return (struct debug_info *)data->debug_info;
+    }
+#else
     return (struct debug_info *)get_thread_data()->debug_info;
+#endif
 }
 
 /* add a string to the output buffer */
diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/env.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/env.c
index 5d2b5252c4..b2ee6a4684 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/env.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/env.c
@@ -2548,5 +2548,15 @@ HANDLE WINAPI PsGetCurrentProcessId(void)
  */
 HANDLE WINAPI PsGetCurrentThreadId(void)
 {
+#ifdef __PROSPERO__
+    /* ⚠ The same thread without Wine's data as in get_info: tid sits at 0x1c
+     * of thread_data, so on the server's own thread this read is of 0x1c. It is
+     * reached from the line after the one get_info is on --
+     * __wine_dbg_header prints GetCurrentThreadId(), which is this -- so fixing
+     * only get_info moved the crash by one line. No thread, no id. */
+    struct thread_data *data = get_thread_data();
+    return ULongToHandle( data ? data->tid : 0 );
+#else
     return ULongToHandle( get_thread_data()->tid );
+#endif
 }
